WEBVTT

00:04.650 --> 00:07.850
Every diesel engine underground puts out exhaust:

00:07.850 --> 00:11.112
gases like carbon monoxide and nitrogen dioxide,

00:11.112 --> 00:13.713
and tiny particles of soot. Those particles are

00:13.713 --> 00:16.825
called diesel particulate matter, or DPM.

00:16.825 --> 00:19.025
They're so small that they float in the air and

00:19.025 --> 00:21.400
get breathed deep into the lungs.

00:22.524 --> 00:26.087
On the surface, exhaust spreads out into open air.

00:26.087 --> 00:28.574
Underground, it builds up in the headings unless

00:28.574 --> 00:31.587
ventilation carries it away. In the short term,

00:31.587 --> 00:33.974
diesel exhaust can irritate your eyes,

00:33.974 --> 00:36.474
nose and throat and cause coughing,

00:36.474 --> 00:39.499
headaches and lightheadedness. Over the long term,

00:39.499 --> 00:42.099
diesel exhaust is classified as a cause of lung

00:42.099 --> 00:45.361
cancer, and it's linked to heart and lung disease.

00:45.361 --> 00:47.587
That's why underground mines have a specific limit

00:47.587 --> 00:48.324
on it.

00:51.646 --> 00:57.284
Under 30 CFR 57.5060, a miner's personal exposure

00:57.284 --> 01:00.984
to DPM in an underground mine can't exceed an average

01:00.984 --> 01:04.696
of 160 micrograms of total carbon per cubic meter

01:04.696 --> 01:07.909
of air over an eight-hour equivalent full shift.

01:07.909 --> 01:10.896
MSHA checks it by sampling, with a pump worn on

01:10.896 --> 01:13.821
the miner's belt. If you're asked to wear a sampler,

01:13.821 --> 01:16.309
wear it the whole shift and work normally.

01:17.440 --> 01:22.390
Other rules control the source. Under 57.5065,

01:22.390 --> 01:25.165
diesel fuel used underground can't have more than

01:25.165 --> 01:29.353
0.05 percent sulfur, and only fuel additives registered

01:29.353 --> 01:34.353
by the EPA can be used. Under 57.5067,

01:34.353 --> 01:37.528
engines brought underground have to be MSHA-approved

01:37.528 --> 01:40.940
or meet EPA particulate limits. Don't add anything

01:40.940 --> 01:43.365
to a fuel tank that you weren't told to add.

01:44.482 --> 01:49.245
Under 57.5066, diesel equipment used underground

01:49.245 --> 01:52.632
has to be kept in approved or manufacturer-specified

01:52.632 --> 01:56.132
condition, including any filters or emission control

01:56.132 --> 01:58.945
devices. And here's the part that's yours.

01:58.945 --> 02:01.832
The operator must authorize every miner who runs

02:01.832 --> 02:04.782
diesel equipment underground to hang a visible,

02:04.782 --> 02:07.095
dated tag on it when there's evidence it needs

02:07.095 --> 02:10.332
maintenance. Evidence means visible smoke or an

02:10.332 --> 02:12.657
odor that's unusual for that machine,

02:12.657 --> 02:16.120
or obvious defects in the exhaust or emission system.

02:16.120 --> 02:18.857
Once tagged, the machine is promptly examined by

02:18.857 --> 02:22.070
a person authorized to maintain diesel equipment,

02:22.070 --> 02:24.345
the tag stays on until that's done,

02:24.345 --> 02:26.557
and it's written in a log. Your instructor will

02:26.557 --> 02:29.695
show you Allen's tags and where the log is kept.

02:30.845 --> 02:33.295
Here's a scenario. A haul truck has been leaving

02:33.295 --> 02:35.745
a black cloud every time it pulls out of the heading

02:35.745 --> 02:38.895
for a week. The driver says it always does that.

02:38.895 --> 02:42.333
Is that a reason to tag it? Yes. Smoke that's unusual

02:42.333 --> 02:45.333
for that machine under normal operation is exactly

02:45.333 --> 02:48.795
the evidence the rule describes. Hang a dated tag,

02:48.795 --> 02:51.583
report it, and let an authorized mechanic examine

02:51.583 --> 02:54.020
it. It may be a plugged air filter,

02:54.020 --> 02:56.920
a bad injector or a damaged exhaust filter.

02:56.920 --> 02:59.320
Every one of those puts more soot in the air you

02:59.320 --> 03:01.558
and your crew breathe all shift.

03:02.686 --> 03:05.386
Your Quarry Equipment Pre-Operational Inspection

03:05.386 --> 03:08.548
is part of DPM control too. Look at the air filter

03:08.548 --> 03:11.486
indicator if the machine has one, listen for exhaust

03:11.486 --> 03:14.348
leaks, and look for damaged or missing parts in

03:14.348 --> 03:16.886
the exhaust or emission control system.

03:16.886 --> 03:19.673
A machine that's hard to start, low on power or

03:19.673 --> 03:22.098
smoking at startup is telling you something.

03:22.098 --> 03:24.786
Write it down and report it, the same as a brake

03:24.786 --> 03:26.061
or a horn problem.

03:29.396 --> 03:32.409
The simplest control is the key. An idling engine

03:32.409 --> 03:35.459
underground makes exhaust and does no work.

03:35.459 --> 03:37.446
If you're going to be waiting, shut it down when

03:37.446 --> 03:39.984
it's safe and allowed to, instead of letting it

03:39.984 --> 03:42.709
run. Don't park a running machine upwind of where

03:42.709 --> 03:45.621
people are working, and don't line trucks up idling

03:45.621 --> 03:46.871
in a dead-end heading.

03:48.002 --> 03:50.627
Ventilation is what carries exhaust out.

03:50.627 --> 03:53.377
The mine fans push fresh air through the workings,

03:53.377 --> 03:55.664
and the air has to reach the places where diesel

03:55.664 --> 03:58.464
equipment works. Don't park equipment or stack

03:58.464 --> 04:01.427
material where it blocks airflow. Don't damage

04:01.427 --> 04:04.552
or leave open any curtain, door or stopping that

04:04.552 --> 04:07.739
directs air. If the air in a heading feels stale

04:07.739 --> 04:10.864
or hazy, or you smell more exhaust than usual,

04:10.864 --> 04:13.039
report it. And if you get a headache,

04:13.039 --> 04:15.977
dizziness or nausea around diesel equipment,

04:15.977 --> 04:18.602
get to fresh air and tell your supervisor.

04:18.602 --> 04:21.289
Carbon monoxide in exhaust can cause those symptoms

04:21.289 --> 04:21.989
too.

04:23.108 --> 04:25.495
If your machine has an enclosed cab with filtered

04:25.495 --> 04:28.058
air, keep the doors and windows shut.

04:28.058 --> 04:31.408
If you're on foot, stand upwind of running equipment,

04:31.408 --> 04:33.970
out of the exhaust plume, and don't hang around

04:33.970 --> 04:36.308
a loading face longer than you need to.

04:37.430 --> 04:40.917
Diesel exhaust is one hazard you can see and smell,

04:40.917 --> 04:43.592
and one you can do a lot about. Don't idle.

04:43.592 --> 04:46.392
Tag a machine that smokes or smells wrong.

04:46.392 --> 04:48.855
Protect the ventilation. Stay out of the exhaust

04:48.855 --> 04:52.030
plume. And if you feel sick around diesel equipment,

04:52.030 --> 04:54.180
get to fresh air and report it.

04:55.304 --> 04:58.466
Remember: your site's plans and procedures always

04:58.466 --> 04:59.966
override this video.
